{"type":"route","meta":{"status":200,"headers":{"cache-control":"public, max-age=3600","content-type":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8","x-robots-tag":"noindex","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/md/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/route,_N_T_/md/how-it-works"}},"body":"# How Amantrika works\n\n> From building your invitation to sharing the link and collecting RSVPs — what happens at each step, and what your guests see.\n\n- Source: http://localhost:3000/how-it-works\n- Last updated: 8 August 2026\n\n---\n## Step one: Build it\n\nYou start with a theme and your details: names, the ceremonies, the venue, a photograph or two. The form asks for things in the order a guest reads them, and it saves as you type — you can stop halfway through and come back.\n\nEverything is optional except the parts a guest actually needs. An invitation with names, a date, a venue and one event is a complete invitation. The story, the gallery, the travel notes and the FAQ are there if you want them and absent if you do not.\n\n## Step two: Change the theme whenever you like\n\nThe content of your invitation and the way it looks are stored separately. That means switching themes does not touch a word of what you have written — you can build the whole invitation, then look at it in four different themes and pick.\n\nThis is deliberate. Most people only know which theme is right once they see their own names in it.\n\n## Step three: Preview it before you pay\n\nBefore publishing, your invitation is fully viewable — the real thing, with a watermark across it. You can send that preview to your family and argue about the wording with everyone looking at the same page.\n\nWhen you publish, the watermark comes off. **The link does not change.** Anything you shared as a preview keeps working.\n\n## Step four: Share one link\n\nYou get a single URL for the invitation. Send it on WhatsApp, put it in an email, print a QR code on the physical card — it is the same link everywhere.\n\nWhat guests see when they open it:\n\n- The invitation itself, designed for a phone first\n- The full schedule, with each function's time and venue\n- A map and one-tap directions\n- A countdown to the next function\n- Numbers they can tap to call\n- An RSVP form that takes about fifteen seconds\n\nNo app, no login, and no third-party tracking script loading on their phone.\n\n## Step five: Collect RSVPs\n\nResponses arrive as a list rather than as replies scattered across family groups. You can see who has responded, how many are coming to which function, and who has not answered yet — which is the part that lets you send a reminder to the twenty people who need one instead of to all four hundred.\n\nYou can export the whole thing to a spreadsheet, because at some point the caterer will ask for a number and you will want to hand them something.\n\n## Step six: Update it when things change\n\nVenues move. Timings shift. The entrance changes to Gate 3 the night before.\n\nEdit the invitation and every guest who already has the link sees the new version. Nothing needs re-sending, and nobody ends up at the old address with a printed card in their hand.\n\n## Afterwards: Afterwards\n\nThe invitation does not have to disappear the day after the wedding. It stays up for a while by default, and can be kept permanently as the place the photographs live — the link your guests already have, still working years later.\n\nThat is the part of the box under the bed we are actually trying to build.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Do my guests need to create an account?\n\nNo. Guests open the link in their phone's browser, read the invitation and RSVP. There is no login, no OTP and no app to install.\n\n### Can I edit the invitation after sharing it?\n\nYes, at any time. The link never changes, so every guest who already has it sees the updated version — which is what makes a late venue or timing change survivable.\n\n### What happens to the invitation after the wedding?\n\nIt stays up for a period after the event date and then becomes an archive page. You can keep it permanently as the place your photographs live.\n"}